\section{Introduction}
Modern high-performance cosmological frameworks face severe performance trade-offs during long-duration execution tracks due to massive disk I/O bottlenecks and decoupling between physics tracking modules and external visual rendering engines. This design breaks those bottlenecks by treating simulation rendering, spatial structural tagging, data serialization, and camera telemetry as a single unified loop. 

\section{Computational Physics & Core Solver Engine}
The engine tracks physical matter distributions over a three-dimensional comoving spatial grid matching standard $\Lambda\text{CDM}$ expansion criteria ($\Omega_m = 0.31$, $\Omega_\Lambda = 0.69$). 

\subsection{Hydrodynamics & Mass Conservation}
Fluid distributions are processed through the compressible Navier-Stokes continuity equations under localized gravitational fields:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot (\rho \mathbf{v}) = 0
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial (\rho \mathbf{v})}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot (\rho \mathbf{v} \otimes \mathbf{v}) + \nabla P = -\rho \nabla \Phi
\end{equation}
where $\rho$ represents baryonic density profiles, $\mathbf{v}$ isolates fluid velocity vectors, $P$ tracks pressure grids, and $\Phi$ represents the gravitational potential field determined via Poisson's equation:
\begin{equation}
\nabla^2 \Phi = 4\pi G a^2 (\rho - \bar{\rho})
\end{equation}

To safeguard the discretization grid against unphysical math divergence errors during collapse acceleration passes, the timestep calculation loop enforces a strict, adaptive Courant-Friedrichs-Lewy (CFL) constraint:
\begin{equation}
\Delta t = C \cdot \frac{\Delta x}{\max(|\mathbf{v}| + c_s)}
\end{equation}
where $C \le 0.25$ tracks the Courant multiplier, $\Delta x$ sets the physical grid voxel width, and $c_s \propto \sqrt{T}$ isolates localized sound speed channels. 

\subsection{Topological Tensor Segmentation}
Structural sorting loops run a localized curvature evaluation loop by assembling a second-order spatial derivative array (The Gravitational Tidal Hessian Tensor Matrix $H_{ij}$):
\begin{equation}
H_{ij} = \frac{\partial^2 \Phi}{\partial x_i \partial x_j}
\end{equation}
Sorting the resulting eigenvalues ($\lambda_1 \ge \lambda_2 \ge \lambda_3$) maps every element into exact geometric categories:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Cluster Nodes}: $\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3 > \lambda_{\text{threshold}}$ (3D Convergence)
    \item \textbf{Filaments}: $\lambda_1, \lambda_2 > \lambda_{\text{threshold}}; \lambda_3 \le \lambda_{\text{threshold}}$ (2D Tubes)
    \item \textbf{Cosmic Sheets}: $\lambda_1 > \lambda_{\text{threshold}}; \lambda_2, \lambda_3 \le \lambda_{\text{threshold}}$ (1D Walls)
    \item \textbf{Voids}: $\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3 \le \lambda_{\text{threshold}}$ (0D Expansion Empty Space)
\end{itemize}

